1
00:00:01,140 --> 00:00:02,042
On day 181,

2
00:00:02,042 --> 00:00:05,268
the loudspeaker gets one more job.

3
00:00:05,268 --> 00:00:06,481
It has called passengers

4
00:00:06,481 --> 00:00:08,368
for Platform 1 for months.

5
00:00:08,368 --> 00:00:10,231
It called the river into your yard.

6
00:00:10,231 --> 00:00:11,369
It called the river's

7
00:00:11,369 --> 00:00:13,063
tail down from the hill.

8
00:00:13,063 --> 00:00:14,873
Now, for the first time,

9
00:00:14,873 --> 00:00:17,632
Nora wants it to say what it was built to say,

10
00:00:17,632 --> 00:00:20,105
and have somebody actually listen.

11
00:00:20,105 --> 00:00:21,742
Your name is Patch.

12
00:00:22,117 --> 00:00:23,316
A hundred and eighty days ago,

13
00:00:23,316 --> 00:00:26,293
you were waiting for a bus.

14
00:00:26,293 --> 00:00:28,751
Since then, you have lived under a bus

15
00:00:28,751 --> 00:00:31,413
depot with Nora, who used to clean it,

16
00:00:31,413 --> 00:00:33,832
Wes, who used to live on a roof,

17
00:00:33,832 --> 00:00:36,365
and since the winter began, a woman from a

18
00:00:36,365 --> 00:00:39,141
truck who still won't tell you her name.

19
00:00:39,141 --> 00:00:41,441
The yard is full of the dead.

20
00:00:41,441 --> 00:00:42,653
So is the street.

21
00:00:42,653 --> 00:00:44,634
In the middle of the yard stands

22
00:00:44,634 --> 00:00:46,492
a coach with a full battery,

23
00:00:46,492 --> 00:00:49,303
its keys hanging on a nail in your bunker,

24
00:00:49,303 --> 00:00:50,954
and eleven passengers standing

25
00:00:50,954 --> 00:00:53,173
between you and the driver's seat.

26
00:00:53,868 --> 00:00:55,676
On day one, that coach called the

27
00:00:55,676 --> 00:00:58,487
whole street into this yard with its alarm.

28
00:00:58,487 --> 00:00:59,854
It seems only fair that it

29
00:00:59,854 --> 00:01:01,775
should take them all out again.

30
00:01:02,373 --> 00:01:04,288
"We open the door," Nora says.

31
00:01:04,288 --> 00:01:06,241
"We turn on the speaker.

32
00:01:06,241 --> 00:01:08,423
And we ask them to get off."

33
00:01:08,423 --> 00:01:09,281
"Will they?"

34
00:01:09,281 --> 00:01:10,360
asks Wes.

35
00:01:10,799 --> 00:01:12,697
"They always did," says Nora.

36
00:01:12,697 --> 00:01:13,811
"Eventually."

37
00:01:14,570 --> 00:01:16,956
The woman looks at the nearly empty racks.

38
00:01:17,378 --> 00:01:19,415
"Eventually is about

39
00:01:19,415 --> 00:01:21,147
four days from now."

40
00:01:21,147 --> 00:01:23,710
Days 182 to 184 are spent

41
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:26,086
sharing out the sunlight.

42
00:01:26,684 --> 00:01:27,306
All winter,

43
00:01:27,306 --> 00:01:30,149
the solar panel has been feeding the coach.

44
00:01:30,149 --> 00:01:33,244
The loudspeaker has gone hungry.

45
00:01:33,244 --> 00:01:36,427
On day 182, Wes unclips the jump leads

46
00:01:36,427 --> 00:01:38,880
from the control box and opens the lid.

47
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:41,175
The little battery light glows.

48
00:01:41,175 --> 00:01:42,262
Barely.

49
00:01:42,262 --> 00:01:43,991
A thin sliver of green.

50
00:01:43,991 --> 00:01:45,721
"How long will it shout?"

51
00:01:45,721 --> 00:01:46,669
you ask.

52
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:49,335
"One announcement," says Wes.

53
00:01:49,335 --> 00:01:50,015
"Maybe two.

54
00:01:50,015 --> 00:01:52,357
Then it's a very expensive box."

55
00:01:52,357 --> 00:01:53,588
So nothing can go

56
00:01:53,588 --> 00:01:55,744
wrong with the first one.

57
00:01:55,744 --> 00:01:57,853
Wes says he should be the one on the coach roof,

58
00:01:57,853 --> 00:01:59,552
because he made the hook.

59
00:01:59,552 --> 00:02:00,890
The woman says she should,

60
00:02:00,890 --> 00:02:03,223
because she is going to drive the thing.

61
00:02:03,694 --> 00:02:05,176
Nora says it will be you,

62
00:02:05,176 --> 00:02:08,740
because you have done it twice and come back both times.

63
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:11,932
Nobody argues with Nora about the coach.

64
00:02:11,932 --> 00:02:13,331
The folding door of the coach

65
00:02:13,331 --> 00:02:14,919
has an outside lever,

66
00:02:14,919 --> 00:02:17,707
low on the front corner, by the steps.

67
00:02:17,707 --> 00:02:19,529
You used it on day 55 to

68
00:02:19,529 --> 00:02:20,882
lock eleven passengers in.

69
00:02:20,882 --> 00:02:23,209
Now you need to let them out.

70
00:02:23,209 --> 00:02:25,338
It is right where the crowd is thickest.

71
00:02:25,338 --> 00:02:27,404
But the hole in the canopy roof is

72
00:02:27,404 --> 00:02:29,554
right above the front of the coach.

73
00:02:29,554 --> 00:02:33,075
And Wes still has the polisher handle.

74
00:02:33,075 --> 00:02:34,840
On day 183,

75
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:37,002
you eat the last tin.

76
00:02:37,002 --> 00:02:39,730
On day 184, you eat the crackers

77
00:02:39,730 --> 00:02:41,951
the woman was hiding for an emergency.

78
00:02:42,309 --> 00:02:43,947
She says this counts.

79
00:02:43,947 --> 00:02:44,840
That night,

80
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:47,750
the stars come out sharp and close again.

81
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:50,416
Nora looks up at them from the gantry.

82
00:02:50,416 --> 00:02:52,195
You know what that means now.

83
00:02:52,922 --> 00:02:54,967
Day 185 is white from

84
00:02:54,967 --> 00:02:57,429
the gate to the canopy.

85
00:02:57,429 --> 00:02:59,680
You go down through the hole onto the

86
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:02,848
coach roof with the rope around your waist.

87
00:03:02,848 --> 00:03:05,392
The dead stand frozen around you like a

88
00:03:05,392 --> 00:03:06,839
garden of statues,

89
00:03:07,213 --> 00:03:11,744
frost on their shoulders, heads turning so slowly that you can't

90
00:03:11,744 --> 00:03:14,625
see them move, only that they have moved.

91
00:03:15,064 --> 00:03:16,343
You crawl to the front

92
00:03:16,343 --> 00:03:18,478
corner of the roof and lie flat.

93
00:03:19,013 --> 00:03:21,398
Below you, the folding door.

94
00:03:21,398 --> 00:03:23,743
Below that, the lever.

95
00:03:23,743 --> 00:03:26,318
Between you and the lever,

96
00:03:26,318 --> 00:03:29,221
a few frozen faces, tilting up.

97
00:03:29,221 --> 00:03:31,666
One frozen hand begins to rise

98
00:03:31,666 --> 00:03:33,557
toward the polisher handle,

99
00:03:33,557 --> 00:03:36,510
as slowly as a plant growing toward a window.

100
00:03:36,510 --> 00:03:39,158
You lower the polisher handle past them.

101
00:03:39,158 --> 00:03:41,854
The wire hook touches the lever.

102
00:03:42,229 --> 00:03:43,273
Slips off.

103
00:03:43,273 --> 00:03:44,698
Touches again.

104
00:03:44,698 --> 00:03:45,852
Catches.

105
00:03:46,628 --> 00:03:47,314
You pull up,

106
00:03:47,314 --> 00:03:50,300
slowly, the way you would lift a sleeping cat.

107
00:03:50,626 --> 00:03:51,802
The lever moves.

108
00:03:51,802 --> 00:03:53,853
With a hiss and a clunk,

109
00:03:53,853 --> 00:03:57,192
the folding door of the coach opens for the

110
00:03:57,192 --> 00:03:59,013
first time in a hundred and thirty days.

111
00:03:59,013 --> 00:04:00,328
Up on the gantry,

112
00:04:00,328 --> 00:04:02,637
Nora flashes the light once.

113
00:04:02,637 --> 00:04:04,526
Wes flips the switch.

114
00:04:04,526 --> 00:04:07,303
"Passengers for Platform 1."

115
00:04:07,303 --> 00:04:08,786
Inside the coach,

116
00:04:08,786 --> 00:04:11,387
eleven heads turn toward the sound.

117
00:04:11,387 --> 00:04:14,014
Ten passengers start down the aisle.

118
00:04:14,014 --> 00:04:15,702
One step at a time.

119
00:04:15,702 --> 00:04:17,597
Down the steps.

120
00:04:17,597 --> 00:04:18,682
Out of the door.

121
00:04:18,682 --> 00:04:20,744
Into the frozen crowd,

122
00:04:20,744 --> 00:04:22,881
toward the voice under the canopy,

123
00:04:22,881 --> 00:04:26,573
like people who have finally heard their bus being called.

124
00:04:26,573 --> 00:04:27,594
Very orderly.

125
00:04:28,049 --> 00:04:29,358
Better behaved than most

126
00:04:29,358 --> 00:04:31,753
passengers you have ever traveled with.

127
00:04:32,448 --> 00:04:33,714
"Mind the step," Wes

128
00:04:33,714 --> 00:04:36,345
whispers from the hole, to nobody.

129
00:04:36,719 --> 00:04:38,587
"Passengers for Plat—" The

130
00:04:38,587 --> 00:04:41,707
speaker dies in the middle of the word.

131
00:04:41,707 --> 00:04:43,248
It doesn't matter.

132
00:04:43,248 --> 00:04:44,321
Ten are off.

133
00:04:44,321 --> 00:04:46,044
But the eleventh is still

134
00:04:46,044 --> 00:04:48,550
standing at the top of the aisle,

135
00:04:48,550 --> 00:04:51,892
behind the driver's seat, as he always has.

136
00:04:51,892 --> 00:04:54,255
The night driver never got off first.

137
00:04:54,255 --> 00:04:56,950
He waited until everybody else was gone.

138
00:04:56,950 --> 00:04:59,535
Nora crawls across the canopy roof to

139
00:04:59,535 --> 00:05:02,313
the front edge, right above the open door.

140
00:05:02,313 --> 00:05:03,984
She doesn't shout.

141
00:05:03,984 --> 00:05:06,877
She just leans out over the edge and says,

142
00:05:06,877 --> 00:05:08,973
very softly, the thing that he used

143
00:05:08,973 --> 00:05:11,693
to say to her every night for nine years.

144
00:05:11,693 --> 00:05:12,646
"Last stop.

145
00:05:12,646 --> 00:05:13,899
All yours."

146
00:05:14,674 --> 00:05:16,014
The man in the blue depot

147
00:05:16,014 --> 00:05:17,508
vest turns toward her voice.

148
00:05:17,508 --> 00:05:18,846
Down the aisle.

149
00:05:18,846 --> 00:05:19,724
Down the steps.

150
00:05:19,724 --> 00:05:22,442
Out of the coach that he drove every night,

151
00:05:22,442 --> 00:05:25,428
into the crowd, his face turned up toward her,

152
00:05:25,428 --> 00:05:27,846
until the crowd closes around him.

153
00:05:27,846 --> 00:05:29,579
Nora stays on the edge of the

154
00:05:29,579 --> 00:05:32,154
canopy roof for a long time after that.

155
00:05:32,154 --> 00:05:33,897
You don't call her back in.

156
00:05:33,897 --> 00:05:35,006
Nobody does.

157
00:05:35,846 --> 00:05:36,711
On day 186,

158
00:05:36,711 --> 00:05:40,099
the woman goes down through the hatch.

159
00:05:40,099 --> 00:05:41,687
She lands in the empty aisle,

160
00:05:41,687 --> 00:05:44,939
steps over the place where the night driver always stood,

161
00:05:44,939 --> 00:05:47,158
and sits down in the driver's seat.

162
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:50,224
She closes the folding door with a button.

163
00:05:50,224 --> 00:05:51,176
Hiss.

164
00:05:51,176 --> 00:05:51,628
Clunk.

165
00:05:51,628 --> 00:05:53,240
Then she sits very still

166
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:55,567
with both hands on the big wheel.

167
00:05:55,567 --> 00:05:56,028
"Well?"

168
00:05:56,028 --> 00:05:58,476
says Wes, through the hatch.

169
00:05:58,835 --> 00:06:01,424
"It smells of old jackets," she says.

170
00:06:01,424 --> 00:06:02,731
"It's perfect."

171
00:06:03,314 --> 00:06:04,594
She checks everything,

172
00:06:04,594 --> 00:06:07,154
the way some people check a sleeping baby.

173
00:06:07,154 --> 00:06:09,106
The lights on the dashboard.

174
00:06:09,106 --> 00:06:09,981
The mirrors.

175
00:06:09,981 --> 00:06:11,238
The fuel gauge.

176
00:06:11,238 --> 00:06:12,854
A quarter of a tank.

177
00:06:12,854 --> 00:06:14,616
"Is that enough?"

178
00:06:14,616 --> 00:06:15,265
you ask.

179
00:06:15,265 --> 00:06:16,634
"Enough to get them

180
00:06:16,634 --> 00:06:18,885
out of town," she says.

181
00:06:18,885 --> 00:06:21,112
"Not enough to get them far away.

182
00:06:21,112 --> 00:06:22,154
We'll see."

183
00:06:22,656 --> 00:06:24,348
Then she walks to the back of

184
00:06:24,348 --> 00:06:25,307
the empty coach,

185
00:06:25,307 --> 00:06:28,088
kneels down and reaches under the last seat.

186
00:06:28,088 --> 00:06:30,709
When she climbs back up through the hatch,

187
00:06:30,709 --> 00:06:32,641
she is holding a small wind-up

188
00:06:32,641 --> 00:06:35,543
travel alarm clock with two bells on top.

189
00:06:35,543 --> 00:06:38,317
"Somebody left this," she says.

190
00:06:38,317 --> 00:06:40,481
"That was me," you say.

191
00:06:40,951 --> 00:06:41,673
"Day 55."

192
00:06:41,673 --> 00:06:44,881
She looks at it, winds it once,

193
00:06:44,881 --> 00:06:47,623
and tucks it into the folds of her scarf.

194
00:06:47,623 --> 00:06:50,342
"Every bus needs a clock."

195
00:06:50,342 --> 00:06:53,875
On day 187, she climbs back up and

196
00:06:53,875 --> 00:06:56,780
tells you the plan for the hundredth time.

197
00:06:56,780 --> 00:06:58,388
You open the gate.

198
00:06:58,388 --> 00:07:00,157
She drives out,

199
00:07:00,157 --> 00:07:01,861
slowly, with the horn going.

200
00:07:01,861 --> 00:07:03,892
The yard follows the coach.

201
00:07:03,892 --> 00:07:05,659
The street follows the yard.

202
00:07:05,659 --> 00:07:07,887
Then she leads them over the

203
00:07:07,887 --> 00:07:09,981
flyover and keeps going until there is a

204
00:07:09,981 --> 00:07:11,581
long downhill road,

205
00:07:11,581 --> 00:07:14,835
and she gives them a last sound somewhere she isn't.

206
00:07:15,514 --> 00:07:16,609
On day 188,

207
00:07:16,609 --> 00:07:20,035
the hill's radio says they are ready.

208
00:07:20,035 --> 00:07:21,647
As soon as the street is empty,

209
00:07:21,647 --> 00:07:24,507
they will walk down the hill with sacks of food.

210
00:07:24,507 --> 00:07:25,842
Nobody in the bunker

211
00:07:25,842 --> 00:07:27,689
has eaten since yesterday.

212
00:07:27,689 --> 00:07:29,345
Wes says he can no longer

213
00:07:29,345 --> 00:07:31,573
remember what food is for.

214
00:07:31,573 --> 00:07:33,712
The woman says that is the first

215
00:07:33,712 --> 00:07:36,661
sensible thing he has said all winter.

216
00:07:36,661 --> 00:07:39,134
You find that the plan sounds

217
00:07:39,134 --> 00:07:42,278
even better on an empty stomach.

218
00:07:42,278 --> 00:07:44,982
On the night of day 189,

219
00:07:44,982 --> 00:07:46,629
everyone takes their place.

220
00:07:46,629 --> 00:07:48,972
The gate bolt has a short loop of rope

221
00:07:48,972 --> 00:07:51,210
tied to its handle, run up to the top

222
00:07:51,210 --> 00:07:53,839
of the canopy ladder, where you will be.

223
00:07:53,839 --> 00:07:55,283
When the coach comes,

224
00:07:55,283 --> 00:07:58,368
you pull the bolt and let the crowd do the pushing.

225
00:07:58,368 --> 00:08:00,596
Nora will be at the far end of

226
00:08:00,596 --> 00:08:02,342
the gantry with the flashlight.

227
00:08:02,342 --> 00:08:04,256
One flash means close it.

228
00:08:04,256 --> 00:08:06,662
Wes will lie at the hole in the canopy roof,

229
00:08:06,662 --> 00:08:07,693
where the woman

230
00:08:07,693 --> 00:08:09,983
can see him through the windshield.

231
00:08:09,983 --> 00:08:13,078
And the woman will sleep in the coach.

232
00:08:13,078 --> 00:08:15,088
"It's got two doors," she says,

233
00:08:15,088 --> 00:08:16,815
climbing down through the

234
00:08:16,815 --> 00:08:18,884
hatch with a blanket under her arm.

235
00:08:18,884 --> 00:08:20,904
"The front one and the roof.

236
00:08:20,904 --> 00:08:22,756
I haven't slept anywhere

237
00:08:22,756 --> 00:08:25,302
with two doors since day four."

238
00:08:25,302 --> 00:08:27,810
You hear her through the hatch,

239
00:08:27,810 --> 00:08:31,205
a long time after the lantern is out, humming.

240
00:08:31,205 --> 00:08:32,248
On the bench,

241
00:08:32,248 --> 00:08:34,892
Nora turns the lantern down low.

242
00:08:34,892 --> 00:08:36,987
"The rule," she says.

243
00:08:37,521 --> 00:08:39,731
"If they come toward the gate,

244
00:08:39,731 --> 00:08:40,762
you close it."

245
00:08:40,762 --> 00:08:43,586
"Even if she's outside," says Nora.

246
00:08:43,586 --> 00:08:46,854
You both know that this time, she will be.

247
00:08:46,854 --> 00:08:48,933
At dawn on day 190,

248
00:08:48,933 --> 00:08:51,210
the woman turns the key.

249
00:08:51,210 --> 00:08:52,916
The engine coughs.

250
00:08:53,305 --> 00:08:54,483
Coughs again.

251
00:08:54,483 --> 00:08:57,551
You think of the truck on day 141,

252
00:08:57,551 --> 00:08:59,227
grinding three streets away,

253
00:08:59,227 --> 00:09:02,737
and every walker in the street turning toward it.

254
00:09:02,737 --> 00:09:04,084
Then the engine catches.

255
00:09:04,084 --> 00:09:05,484
Through the windshield,

256
00:09:05,484 --> 00:09:08,947
you see the woman pull her goggles down over her eyes.

257
00:09:08,947 --> 00:09:10,130
Up at the hole,

258
00:09:10,130 --> 00:09:13,531
Wes gives her a thumbs up with his mitten.

259
00:09:13,531 --> 00:09:15,660
A deep diesel rumble rolls out under

260
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:18,044
the canopy and fills the whole yard.

261
00:09:18,044 --> 00:09:19,598
Every head in the depot

262
00:09:19,598 --> 00:09:21,250
turns toward the coach.

263
00:09:21,250 --> 00:09:22,781
She leans on the horn.

264
00:09:22,781 --> 00:09:24,089
Not a short blast.

265
00:09:24,089 --> 00:09:24,935
A long one,

266
00:09:24,935 --> 00:09:27,489
the one that empties a flyover.

267
00:09:28,071 --> 00:09:29,666
The coach starts to move.

268
00:09:29,666 --> 00:09:31,017
It moves at the speed

269
00:09:31,017 --> 00:09:32,389
of a very slow queue.

270
00:09:32,389 --> 00:09:34,932
The dead in front of it don't fall.

271
00:09:35,386 --> 00:09:37,692
They turn to face it and walk backward,

272
00:09:37,692 --> 00:09:39,856
their faces against the windshield,

273
00:09:39,856 --> 00:09:41,407
their hands on the glass,

274
00:09:41,407 --> 00:09:43,762
as if the coach is something they

275
00:09:43,762 --> 00:09:45,639
have been waiting for all their lives.

276
00:09:45,639 --> 00:09:47,452
The crowd around it presses in,

277
00:09:47,452 --> 00:09:49,632
walks with it, follows it.

278
00:09:50,071 --> 00:09:51,782
The whole yard begins to turn,

279
00:09:51,782 --> 00:09:53,744
like water around a plughole.

280
00:09:54,312 --> 00:09:55,811
From the top of the ladder,

281
00:09:55,811 --> 00:09:58,880
you watch the crowd at the gate lean the other way.

282
00:09:58,880 --> 00:10:00,103
Toward the horn.

283
00:10:00,103 --> 00:10:01,842
Away from the gate.

284
00:10:02,458 --> 00:10:05,013
A gap opens along the inside of the wall.

285
00:10:05,013 --> 00:10:06,469
You pull the rope.

286
00:10:06,924 --> 00:10:08,891
The bolt lifts out of its hole.

287
00:10:08,891 --> 00:10:11,943
And the gate, oiled all summer by Wes,

288
00:10:11,943 --> 00:10:14,758
pushed all winter by the street,

289
00:10:14,758 --> 00:10:17,595
slides open with a polite little whisper.

290
00:10:17,595 --> 00:10:18,930
A hand's width.

291
00:10:18,930 --> 00:10:20,920
Then a body's width.

292
00:10:20,920 --> 00:10:22,740
Then wide open.

293
00:10:22,740 --> 00:10:24,856
The street pours into the gateway,

294
00:10:24,856 --> 00:10:26,302
toward the horn,

295
00:10:26,302 --> 00:10:28,618
and meets the yard coming the other way.

296
00:10:28,618 --> 00:10:31,409
For one long minute, nothing moves at all.

297
00:10:31,409 --> 00:10:33,027
You hang on to the top of the

298
00:10:33,027 --> 00:10:35,162
ladder with the rope in your fist,

299
00:10:35,162 --> 00:10:37,812
and a few hundred faces press past the

300
00:10:37,812 --> 00:10:39,881
foot of it, close enough to touch your shoes,

301
00:10:39,881 --> 00:10:41,194
none of them looking up.

302
00:10:41,194 --> 00:10:43,841
Then the coach noses into the gateway,

303
00:10:43,841 --> 00:10:45,499
slow and enormous,

304
00:10:45,499 --> 00:10:48,808
and the crowd in front of it gives way into the street,

305
00:10:48,808 --> 00:10:50,804
turns, and starts to walk.

306
00:10:50,804 --> 00:10:53,277
The coach rolls out of the gate.

307
00:10:53,277 --> 00:10:54,114
It turns downhill,

308
00:10:54,114 --> 00:10:56,744
toward the lower town, toward the flyover.

309
00:10:56,744 --> 00:10:59,230
Behind it, the yard follows.

310
00:10:59,230 --> 00:11:01,326
Behind the yard, the street.

311
00:11:01,326 --> 00:11:03,925
Above them all, the sign over the

312
00:11:03,925 --> 00:11:05,742
street swings in the wind of a few

313
00:11:05,742 --> 00:11:08,111
thousand bodies passing underneath it.

314
00:11:08,111 --> 00:11:09,421
It takes all day.

315
00:11:09,421 --> 00:11:11,323
They pour out of the

316
00:11:11,323 --> 00:11:12,908
yard like sand out of a bag.

317
00:11:12,908 --> 00:11:15,197
The ones from under the canopy.

318
00:11:15,197 --> 00:11:17,226
The ones from around the bay door.

319
00:11:17,226 --> 00:11:19,064
The ones who have been standing

320
00:11:19,064 --> 00:11:21,687
against your ceiling since the end of summer.

321
00:11:22,970 --> 00:11:25,049
Hour after hour, they walk out of the

322
00:11:25,049 --> 00:11:26,994
gate and down the street,

323
00:11:26,994 --> 00:11:29,819
after the sound of a horn that is getting farther away.

324
00:11:30,166 --> 00:11:31,738
The bay door stops creaking.

325
00:11:31,738 --> 00:11:33,447
The canopy stops echoing.

326
00:11:33,976 --> 00:11:34,653
Bit by bit,

327
00:11:34,653 --> 00:11:38,451
the depot remembers what it sounds like when it is empty.

328
00:11:38,451 --> 00:11:39,812
Late in the afternoon,

329
00:11:39,812 --> 00:11:41,927
the yard is almost empty.

330
00:11:41,927 --> 00:11:43,565
A few stragglers.

331
00:11:43,565 --> 00:11:44,293
Then two.

332
00:11:44,293 --> 00:11:45,299
Then one.

333
00:11:45,839 --> 00:11:47,579
The last one out of the yard wears

334
00:11:47,579 --> 00:11:49,846
a blue depot vest with two pockets.

335
00:11:49,846 --> 00:11:51,785
He walks slowly through the gateway,

336
00:11:51,785 --> 00:11:53,080
following his bus.

337
00:11:53,613 --> 00:11:54,578
And in the street,

338
00:11:54,578 --> 00:11:57,464
standing beside the gate with his hands at his sides,

339
00:11:57,464 --> 00:11:59,871
there is a walker in an olive vest,

340
00:11:59,871 --> 00:12:01,443
who waits for him to pass,

341
00:12:01,443 --> 00:12:04,310
like a doorman, and then follows him down the hill.

342
00:12:04,765 --> 00:12:06,108
Up on the gantry,

343
00:12:06,108 --> 00:12:08,196
a small light flashes once.

344
00:12:08,196 --> 00:12:11,076
You climb down the ladder into an empty yard,

345
00:12:11,076 --> 00:12:13,448
slide the gate shut and drop the bolt.

346
00:12:13,448 --> 00:12:14,557
It is the quietest

347
00:12:14,557 --> 00:12:16,375
sound you have ever made.

348
00:12:16,911 --> 00:12:20,360
On day 191, Nora walks into the yard.

349
00:12:20,670 --> 00:12:21,854
Not to the coach.

350
00:12:21,854 --> 00:12:23,103
There is no coach.

351
00:12:23,103 --> 00:12:24,521
Not to the gate.

352
00:12:24,864 --> 00:12:26,750
Just into the middle of the concrete,

353
00:12:26,750 --> 00:12:27,943
where the sun is.

354
00:12:28,462 --> 00:12:30,085
She sits down on the ground,

355
00:12:30,085 --> 00:12:33,778
in the daylight, with her back against a canopy pillar,

356
00:12:33,778 --> 00:12:35,025
and closes her eyes.

357
00:12:35,025 --> 00:12:37,384
You sit down a little way off.

358
00:12:37,384 --> 00:12:38,366
You don't talk.

359
00:12:38,366 --> 00:12:40,267
Nothing turns toward you.

360
00:12:40,267 --> 00:12:41,460
Nobody watches.

361
00:12:41,460 --> 00:12:44,311
There is a dark rectangle on the

362
00:12:44,311 --> 00:12:46,371
concrete where the coach stood for

363
00:12:46,371 --> 00:12:46,959
a hundred and ninety days,

364
00:12:46,959 --> 00:12:49,786
and the only sound in the yard is the wind.

365
00:12:49,786 --> 00:12:50,765
After a long time,

366
00:12:50,765 --> 00:12:52,278
without opening her eyes,

367
00:12:52,278 --> 00:12:55,133
Nora says, "It's warmer than I remembered."

368
00:12:55,781 --> 00:12:57,795
At sunset, the woman's voice comes

369
00:12:57,795 --> 00:13:00,224
on the radio, faint and crackling.

370
00:13:00,224 --> 00:13:01,715
"Over the flyover.

371
00:13:01,715 --> 00:13:02,875
Still coming.

372
00:13:02,875 --> 00:13:03,917
All of them."

373
00:13:03,917 --> 00:13:07,500
In the morning, Wes climbs down the ladder,

374
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:09,936
walks once around the whole empty yard,

375
00:13:09,936 --> 00:13:11,514
touches the gate,

376
00:13:11,514 --> 00:13:14,991
touches every canopy pillar, and then runs in

377
00:13:14,991 --> 00:13:17,676
a big slow circle with his arms out,

378
00:13:17,676 --> 00:13:19,872
laughing without a sound,

379
00:13:19,872 --> 00:13:22,940
like a dog that has finally been let off the lead.

380
00:13:23,315 --> 00:13:26,794
On day 192, the same.

381
00:13:27,473 --> 00:13:30,382
On day 193, she says the tail was still

382
00:13:30,382 --> 00:13:33,294
climbing the ramp when she reached the top.

383
00:13:33,294 --> 00:13:34,451
She couldn't stop.

384
00:13:34,451 --> 00:13:37,159
She is following the motorway toward the city,

385
00:13:37,159 --> 00:13:39,318
looking for a long downhill road.

386
00:13:39,917 --> 00:13:41,120
"I'm taking them back

387
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:43,252
where they came from," she says.

388
00:13:43,610 --> 00:13:46,131
"Return to sender."

389
00:13:46,131 --> 00:13:48,112
On day 194,

390
00:13:48,438 --> 00:13:50,760
there are footsteps in the street.

391
00:13:50,760 --> 00:13:51,623
Living ones.

392
00:13:51,623 --> 00:13:53,892
From the gantry, you watch a little

393
00:13:53,892 --> 00:13:56,312
line of people walk down the hill road,

394
00:13:56,312 --> 00:13:59,177
through the split and into your street,

395
00:13:59,177 --> 00:14:01,125
carrying sacks on their shoulders.

396
00:14:01,125 --> 00:14:02,461
Every one of them has a

397
00:14:02,461 --> 00:14:04,256
blue bandage somewhere.

398
00:14:04,256 --> 00:14:05,449
They stop at the gate.

399
00:14:05,449 --> 00:14:06,935
One of them knocks.

400
00:14:06,935 --> 00:14:07,674
Three times.

401
00:14:07,674 --> 00:14:10,025
Then a fourth time, softer.

402
00:14:10,398 --> 00:14:12,638
Somebody has been talking on the radio.

403
00:14:12,638 --> 00:14:14,002
Nora looks at you.

404
00:14:14,408 --> 00:14:15,701
You look at Nora.

405
00:14:15,701 --> 00:14:17,354
Then you open the gate,

406
00:14:17,354 --> 00:14:19,652
all the way, for the living.

407
00:14:19,652 --> 00:14:22,261
That night, in the middle of the yard,

408
00:14:22,261 --> 00:14:24,635
you build a fire out of an old wooden pallet,

409
00:14:24,635 --> 00:14:27,013
and the hill people bake potatoes in it.

410
00:14:27,013 --> 00:14:29,326
It is the first hot food you have

411
00:14:29,326 --> 00:14:31,850
eaten since you were waiting for a bus.

412
00:14:31,850 --> 00:14:33,223
Nobody whispers.

413
00:14:33,597 --> 00:14:35,129
The fire crackles,

414
00:14:35,129 --> 00:14:37,682
and nothing comes to see why.

415
00:14:37,682 --> 00:14:39,542
On days 195 and 196,

416
00:14:39,542 --> 00:14:43,545
the hill people sleep in the corridor and the store,

417
00:14:43,545 --> 00:14:46,630
and walk around the depot as if it is a museum.

418
00:14:46,988 --> 00:14:49,170
Wes shows them the bay door latch.

419
00:14:49,170 --> 00:14:49,906
Twice.

420
00:14:50,457 --> 00:14:52,055
Then he takes the pencil and adds

421
00:14:52,055 --> 00:14:53,371
a whole row of marks to the

422
00:14:53,371 --> 00:14:55,781
people met column, one at a time,

423
00:14:55,781 --> 00:15:00,347
very slowly, enjoying every single one.

424
00:15:00,347 --> 00:15:03,777
On day 197, the woman's voice is fainter.

425
00:15:03,777 --> 00:15:06,032
The motorway is long and flat.

426
00:15:06,032 --> 00:15:07,255
No downhill yet.

427
00:15:07,255 --> 00:15:10,653
Every sunset, Nora says, "Still here."

428
00:15:10,653 --> 00:15:13,627
Every sunset, the woman says it back.

429
00:15:14,066 --> 00:15:16,004
Each time, it is a little quieter,

430
00:15:16,004 --> 00:15:18,254
as if she is talking from the bottom

431
00:15:18,254 --> 00:15:20,543
of a well that keeps getting deeper.

432
00:15:20,543 --> 00:15:24,001
On day 199, fainter still.

433
00:15:24,001 --> 00:15:27,335
"The fuel light's on," she says.

434
00:15:27,335 --> 00:15:28,864
"Don't tell anybody."

435
00:15:28,864 --> 00:15:29,878
On day 201,

436
00:15:29,878 --> 00:15:31,730
you can barely hear her at all.

437
00:15:31,730 --> 00:15:33,749
Something about a bridge.

438
00:15:33,749 --> 00:15:35,104
Something about the city's

439
00:15:35,104 --> 00:15:36,909
first houses in the distance.

440
00:15:37,428 --> 00:15:39,461
Then a long hiss.

441
00:15:39,461 --> 00:15:41,990
On day 203, nothing.

442
00:15:41,990 --> 00:15:45,395
From day 204 to day 209,

443
00:15:45,395 --> 00:15:47,246
the radio says nothing at all.

444
00:15:47,246 --> 00:15:49,330
You lie awake in the bunker,

445
00:15:49,330 --> 00:15:51,532
under a ceiling with no footsteps on it,

446
00:15:51,532 --> 00:15:53,855
and think about a woman sitting in a bus

447
00:15:53,855 --> 00:15:56,629
on a motorway, in the middle of the whole river,

448
00:15:56,629 --> 00:15:58,406
with a clock in her scarf

449
00:15:58,406 --> 00:16:00,168
and a quarter of nothing in her tank.

450
00:16:00,168 --> 00:16:02,408
You leave them on at sunset anyway.

451
00:16:02,408 --> 00:16:03,716
Both of them.

452
00:16:04,187 --> 00:16:05,913
The depot's and the hill's,

453
00:16:05,913 --> 00:16:08,185
side by side on the rack,

454
00:16:08,185 --> 00:16:10,654
hissing at the same empty channel.

455
00:16:10,654 --> 00:16:13,194
The hill people go back up the hill.

456
00:16:13,194 --> 00:16:14,940
The yard stays empty.

457
00:16:15,427 --> 00:16:16,941
The gate stays shut.

458
00:16:16,941 --> 00:16:17,878
Every night,

459
00:16:17,878 --> 00:16:20,827
Nora climbs to the end of the gantry and flashes

460
00:16:20,827 --> 00:16:22,572
the light toward the motorway.

461
00:16:22,572 --> 00:16:23,201
Twice.

462
00:16:23,201 --> 00:16:25,087
Once for still here.

463
00:16:25,087 --> 00:16:26,644
Once for her.

464
00:16:27,387 --> 00:16:29,384
On day 206, Wes walks the three

465
00:16:29,384 --> 00:16:32,987
streets to where the truck died on day 141.

466
00:16:32,987 --> 00:16:34,831
It is still there,

467
00:16:34,831 --> 00:16:37,425
white with frost, with its cab door open.

468
00:16:37,425 --> 00:16:39,006
Its battery is dead.

469
00:16:39,006 --> 00:16:40,859
Its tank is half full.

470
00:16:40,859 --> 00:16:43,574
He comes back and doesn't say anything,

471
00:16:43,574 --> 00:16:44,242
but he looks at

472
00:16:44,242 --> 00:16:45,880
the jump leads for a long time.

473
00:16:46,463 --> 00:16:47,409
Up on the gantry,

474
00:16:47,409 --> 00:16:50,589
the little battery light in the control box creeps

475
00:16:50,589 --> 00:16:52,779
back toward green, a sliver at a time.

476
00:16:52,779 --> 00:16:54,932
The loudspeaker is eating again.

477
00:16:55,467 --> 00:16:56,287
On day 208,

478
00:16:56,287 --> 00:16:59,240
Nora opens the tin of white paint.

479
00:16:59,240 --> 00:17:01,064
She looks at the sign for a while.

480
00:17:01,064 --> 00:17:02,719
Then she closes it again.

481
00:17:03,334 --> 00:17:06,503
"Not until she tells us," she says.

482
00:17:06,503 --> 00:17:09,536
On day 210, at sunset, the radio crackles.

483
00:17:09,536 --> 00:17:12,462
Everyone in the bunker stops breathing.

484
00:17:12,462 --> 00:17:12,895
"Depot."

485
00:17:12,895 --> 00:17:13,947
It is the woman,

486
00:17:13,947 --> 00:17:16,188
so faint that it is mostly hiss.

487
00:17:16,188 --> 00:17:18,004
"The coach is out of fuel.

488
00:17:18,004 --> 00:17:18,890
I'm inside.

489
00:17:18,890 --> 00:17:20,300
They're all around it.

490
00:17:20,300 --> 00:17:21,097
All of them."

491
00:17:21,097 --> 00:17:22,987
Nora grabs the radio.

492
00:17:22,987 --> 00:17:23,997
"Where are you?"

493
00:17:23,997 --> 00:17:24,670
"Motorway.

494
00:17:24,670 --> 00:17:26,104
Past the bridge.

495
00:17:26,104 --> 00:17:27,320
Before the city."

496
00:17:27,320 --> 00:17:28,465
A long crackle.

497
00:17:28,465 --> 00:17:30,945
"The battery's almost gone.

498
00:17:30,945 --> 00:17:31,424
Nora.

499
00:17:31,424 --> 00:17:33,496
You asked me for my name."

500
00:17:33,496 --> 00:17:34,195
"Yes."

501
00:17:34,680 --> 00:17:37,099
"It's—" The hiss swallows the rest.

502
00:17:37,099 --> 00:17:38,395
Then nothing.

503
00:17:39,281 --> 00:17:40,631
"Say it again," Nora

504
00:17:40,631 --> 00:17:42,707
whispers into the radio.

505
00:17:43,129 --> 00:17:44,269
"Say it again."

506
00:17:44,269 --> 00:17:47,604
The channel hisses back at her, empty.

507
00:17:47,604 --> 00:17:49,561
Wes picks up the flashlight and goes

508
00:17:49,561 --> 00:17:51,784
up to the gantry without a word,

509
00:17:51,784 --> 00:17:53,686
and you hear him on the metal steps,

510
00:17:53,686 --> 00:17:56,154
climbing toward the motorway side of the

511
00:17:56,154 --> 00:17:58,044
depot to flash it twice into the dark.

512
00:17:58,044 --> 00:18:00,373
A hundred and ninety days in the dark,

513
00:18:00,373 --> 00:18:02,689
and twenty days in daylight.

514
00:18:02,689 --> 00:18:04,669
The same orange bandage.

515
00:18:05,123 --> 00:18:06,954
Nora is holding the radio.

516
00:18:06,954 --> 00:18:09,337
You are holding the orange bag.

517
00:18:09,337 --> 00:18:11,594
Tomorrow is day 211.

518
00:18:11,594 --> 00:18:13,868
Your yard is empty.

519
00:18:13,868 --> 00:18:15,477
Your gate is your own.

520
00:18:15,477 --> 00:18:17,719
And somewhere down the motorway,

521
00:18:17,719 --> 00:18:20,837
the whole river is standing around one bus,

522
00:18:20,837 --> 00:18:22,970
with a woman inside who has finally

523
00:18:22,970 --> 00:18:25,057
found a place with one way out,

524
00:18:25,057 --> 00:18:29,004
and who just told you her name a second too late.

525
00:18:29,004 --> 00:18:30,604
Would you have opened the gate?

526
00:18:30,604 --> 00:18:31,653
Which decision would

527
00:18:31,653 --> 00:18:33,597
you have made differently?

528
00:18:33,597 --> 00:18:35,128
Tell us in the comments,

529
00:18:35,128 --> 00:18:37,088
and subscribe to Last Safe Door,

530
00:18:37,088 --> 00:18:39,534
so you're here for day 211.
